            <tr><td><strong>Combined / Provisional Income</strong></td><td><strong>$13,000</strong> (Under $25,000 threshold)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxable Portion of Social Security</td><td><strong>$0 (0% Taxable — 100% Tax-Free)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Estimated Federal Income Tax</strong></td><td><strong>$0</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sstc-ex-note"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Seniors whose sole financial support is Social Security pay zero federal income tax on their benefits.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Example 2 -->
    <div class="sstc-example-card">
      <div class="sstc-example-header">
        <span class="sstc-ex-badge">Example 2</span>
        <h3>Single Retiree — Social Security + Moderate Pension ($22,000 SS + $20,000 Pension)</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="sstc-table-wrap">
        <table class="sstc-table-sm">
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Gross Social Security Benefit</td><td>$22,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pension Income</td><td>$20,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50% of Social Security Benefit</td><td>$11,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Combined / Provisional Income</strong></td><td><strong>$31,000</strong> ($20k + $11k)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxability Tier</td><td>50% Tier ($25,000 to $34,000 range)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxable Social Security (50% of excess over $25k)</td><td><strong>$3,000</strong> (13.6% of benefit)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Adjusted Gross Income (AGI)</td><td>$23,000 ($20k pension + $3k taxable SS)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (Age 65+)</td><td>($18,150) ($16.1k base + $2.05k senior)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Federal Taxable Income</td><td>$4,850 (taxed at 10%)</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Estimated Federal Income Tax</strong></td><td><strong>$485</strong> (1.1% effective tax rate)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sstc-ex-note"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Even though $3,000 of Social Security is taxable, the $18,150 senior standard deduction keeps total federal tax at just $485.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Example 3 -->
    <div class="sstc-example-card">
      <div class="sstc-example-header">
        <span class="sstc-ex-badge">Example 3</span>
