        <li>
          <span class="sstc-check-icon">✓</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Step 3: Check Your Proximity to the $25k/$32k or $34k/$44k Thresholds</strong>
            <p>If you are within $1,000 or $2,000 of a threshold, delaying a non-essential withdrawal until January can keep your benefits in a lower tax tier.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sstc-check-icon">✓</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Step 4: Submit IRS Form W-4V If You Owe More Than $1,000</strong>
            <p>Download <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/fw4v.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link" style="color: #FFFFFF;">IRS Form W-4V</a>, check Box 6 for 7% or 10% withholding, and mail it to your local Social Security office to spread payments automatically.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sstc-check-icon">✓</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Step 5: Use QCDs If You Are Age 70½+ and Giving to Charity</strong>
            <p>Instruct your IRA custodian to send charitable gifts directly to 501(c)(3) nonprofits. This satisfies your RMD without increasing your provisional income.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sstc-check-icon">✓</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Step 6: Check Your State Tax Exemption</strong>
